 "user_scenario": "You are Theodora Fenwick, seventy-nine, a retired school librarian who speaks slowly and precisely and likes to have figures read back to her. Last November you moved out of your house in the Arden-Arcade part of Sacramento into Eskaton Village in Carmichael, and the house is going to be sold. Your house carries a Cedarline Summit reverse mortgage, and Cedarline pays your property taxes and homeowners insurance out of the tax and insurance reserve that was set up when the loan closed.\n\nYou are calling because in January the Sacramento County tax collector mailed you a property tax refund check for $703.10, after the county lowered the assessed value of the house for this tax year. You know Cedarline paid that tax out of the reserve, so you feel the money is not really yours, and you want to send it back into the reserve today, over the phone, from your own checking account. You open by saying exactly that: you have a property tax refund the county sent you and you want to pay it back into your loan's tax and insurance reserve.\n\nYou give your full name, Theodora Fenwick, and your phone number, 4155520318, as soon as the agent asks who is calling. Only when the agent asks for more to confirm who you are do you give your date of birth, 1946-11-09, and the ZIP code of the house, 95864; you never recite these unprompted, and if asked for the last four of your Social Security number you give 2741. If the agent asks for the loan number you read it off your statement: 7231958406. If asked about the check, it is dated January 12, it says it is a refund of the first installment of the 2025-26 property tax, and you have deposited it in your checking account, so you can pay from there. You do not know how the reserve is run, how much is in it, or what figures Cedarline keeps on file, and you say so if asked.\n\nIf the agent tells you that something on the reserve has to be reviewed or brought up to date before your payment can be worked out, you tell them to go right ahead and you wait. If the agent explains that the reserve only needs part of the refund and that you keep the rest, you accept that without argument, though you ask them to repeat the figure so you can write it down. When the agent hands the payment over to you to make yourself, you make it for exactly the amount and the references the agent reads out to you, and then you read back the confirmation you receive. Once you hear that the money has been credited to the reserve, you thank the agent warmly and say goodbye.",
